From 3c357732e6e2d9b030e0c3f208bbd50ddb70ed27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 17:26:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(viz): suppress a canvas quad only once its resource is
 proven openable
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The lockstep invariant, in shared code: NEVER SUPPRESS A REGION YOU CANNOT DRAW
BACK. Display already withheld a weave rect for a tile with no resolved canvas
layer; this closes the other half, which the device capture showed wide open —
"recovery draw: n=0 missing=10 ... [no_mailbox=0 produce=10]". Ten layers that
all RESOLVED, all got suppressed, and none of which could be opened. Every one
of those tiles was a hole with nothing behind it.

The predicate was too weak. "The ResourceId resolved to a non-zero mailbox" is
what Display can see at aggregation time, and both platforms fail DOWNSTREAM of
exactly that — Android refuses at ProduceSkia, Windows at BeginScopedReadAccess.
Only the GPU thread can establish the predicate that matters, and it does so
after Display has already decided. So Display now consults evidence the GPU
thread writes: a lock-guarded, token-keyed set of elements whose canvas resource
has been opened successfully at least once (Inline3dRecoveryUsable, defined in
skia_output_surface.cc — both parties are in the GPU process on different
threads, so a lock is the whole channel).

Three parts, and the third is what makes it converge rather than latch:

  * Display splits resolved layers into SUPPRESSED (proven) and PROBE (not yet
    proven). Only the proven set reaches SetInline3dSuppressedResources.
  * |inline_3d_canvas_layers_| still means EXACTLY the suppressed set, so every
    "is |output| at this rect a hole?" test is untouched. The probe list is a
    separate third list that is never a weave source and never answers that
    question.
  * The GPU stage opens the probe layers once per frame purely to learn. Without
    it an unproven layer is never opened, so it never becomes proven, so it is
    never suppressed — a tile that failed once would stay flat forever. Cost is
    zero in the steady state: a layer that opens is promoted and the list
    empties.

The rect is ALWAYS submitted, for both lists. Withholding it would stop the
weave running for that tile, and the weave's own successful canvas open is one
of the two ways a layer gets promoted. Not suppressing it is what makes that
safe: the page drew the quad, so the weave draws over real pixels, never a hole.
Worst case is one frame of the page's own side-by-side. Flat is acceptable,
empty never is.

Demotion is recorded by the only code entitled to an opinion: the recovery draw,
at the moment it tries to fill a hole and cannot.

Also folded in, free, the two hypotheses for the shared root cause — the failure
is DETERMINISTIC (produce=N/N every frame on Android, read_access=N/N on
Windows), which rules out contention and points at a static mismatch. On the
first recovery failure only, [DXR-H-PROBE] asks the SAME mailbox for an OVERLAY
representation and reports whether the layer took part in this frame's weave.
Overlay OK where Skia refused => H1, a representation/usage mismatch at
creation, and the fix is a usage bit rather than a new primitive. A layer that
was never submitted and still fails refutes H2 for it — and note the weave
path's own access is scoped to its compose block and released long before
recovery runs, and canvas mailboxes are never entered into
|skia_representations_|, so nothing caches an access across the frame either.

